[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the app determine which environment it's running in? → Hostname + override (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Hostname with an override it is.

  [1mSecond question: what shape should the settings module take?[0m

  The repo has two conventions and they don't mix: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a browser global script, [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m is CommonJS for Node. The
  settings module has to pick one, and that picks who can consume it later.

  - [1mBrowser global script[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m at the root, loaded by [38;5;153mindex.html[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing something like
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m. Buys: matches how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already works, zero other changes, opens from [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Costs: [38;5;153msrc/[39m Node
    code can't [38;5;153mrequire[39m it without a compatibility shim; globals don't scale past a handful of settings.
  - [1mES module[0m — add [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m to the script tag and use real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Buys: the modern default, explicit
    dependencies, no globals, and it's the direction you'd grow toward anyway. Costs: [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m means the page must
    be served over HTTP — opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly from disk stops working. That's the tradeoff that bites.
  - [1mDual CommonJS/browser file in [38;5;153msrc/[0m — one file with an export guard both sides can load. Buys: single source of truth
    shared with the Node entry point. Costs: shim boilerplate for a sharing need that doesn't exist yet — [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m
    makes no API calls at all. This is the YAGNI option.

  My recommendation is the browser global script. It's the smallest change that matches existing code, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/[39m share nothing today, so designing for reuse between them would be inventing a requirement.
